    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d18">

                <pb facs="0029" n="19" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg19"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01/5–1049: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Australia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Foster</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Canberra</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-05-10T16:00:00+10:00">May 10, 1949—4 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 11—3 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>99. Saw Burton<anchor n="37" xml:id="d18-f1"/> today and urged position outlined
                    Depcirtel May 6, 5 p. m. Burton said Australia planned continue its Embassy in
                    Nanking and had no intention at present recognizing Communists in China. Added
                    however that circumstances might change and that Australia could give no
                    commitments for future.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Foster</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d18-f1" n="37" xml:id="d18-f1-content">
                <p>John W. Burton, Secretary of the Australian Department of External Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
